  <div class="col-lg-9">
    <div class="d-flex flex-column gap-4">

      <article id="origins" class="changelog-entry p-4">
        <div class="index-chapter-era font-monospace mb-1">Background</div>
        <h2 class="h4 fw-semibold mb-3">Origins</h2>
        <div class="index-prose">
          <p>
            With its origins going back to the late 1970s, The Scene comes from an era when access to the Internet
            was restricted and the other major computer networks were inaccessible to the general public.
            Instead, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bulletin_board_system" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bulletin Board Systems</a> hosted on personal computers housed in
            people's homes and connected to the copper voice-telephone network took on this online role for many savvy, early computer owners.
            Running specialized software and hardware, these boards enabled like-minded people to use their home computers to
            connect, communicate, and later exchange digital files.
          </p>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article id="the-naughty" class="changelog-entry p-4">
        <div class="index-chapter-era font-monospace mb-1">The naughty</div>
        <h2 class="h4 fw-semibold mb-3">Underground Scenes</h2>

        <figure class="border-start border-3 border-primary ps-3 py-1 mb-4 index-prose">
          <blockquote class="mb-2 fst-italic">
            <p class="mb-0">"An anarchically governed free-for-all that has nonetheless developed its own codes of behavior,
            ethics, activities, and most importantly, hierarchies of prestige."</p>
          </blockquote>
          <figcaption class="small text-body-secondary mb-0">
            <a href="https://eve.gd/books/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Martin Paul Eve</a>, <cite title="Warez: The Infrastructure and Aesthetics of Piracy">Warez: The Infrastructure and Aesthetics of Piracy</cite>
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <div class="d-flex flex-column gap-3">
          <section class="border rounded-3 p-3 bg-body-tertiary">
            <h3 class="h5 fw-semibold mb-1">Crack</h3>
            <div class="font-monospace small text-body-secondary mb-2">cracking &middot; training &middot; pirating &middot; wares &middot; warez</div>
            <div class="index-prose small">
              <p>
                Cracking is the craft of modifying software for a particular purpose.
                It can vary widely from the simple act of <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trainer_(games)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">creating cheats for games</a> (trainers)
                to the removal of <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Copy_protection" target="_blank" rel="noopener">sophisticated software copy protection</a>.
                The sharing of commercial software, often accompanied by the removal of copy protection,
                <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Warez" target="_blank" rel="noopener">is defined as <strong>warez</strong></a>.
                But software cracking and warez were not always the same, or even illegal.
              </p>
              <p>
                Until December 1980, authors in the US could not use copyright to restrict program usage on a computer, as software got defined as a literary work like a book.
                Even after the missing law was rectified, it was uncertain if the copyright controls applied to the author's source code or also to the compiled program.
                However, other than the existing counterfeit and trademark laws, there were few legal restrictions on the use or modification of the software after it was in the hands of a customer.
              </p>
              <p>
                This situation changed in the late 1990s and 2000s with the rise of the Internet,
                entertainment media moving to digital formats, and excessive lobbying by the US entertainment and media industries.
                After which, one could argue with today's "copyright&nbsp;trolls", the law overcorrected,
                giving rights holders excessive legal power over media at the expense of customers and public fair use.
              </p>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section class="border rounded-3 p-3 bg-body-tertiary">
            <h3 class="h5 fw-semibold mb-1">Hack</h3>
            <div class="font-monospace small text-body-secondary mb-2">hacking &middot; hpav &middot; computer phreaking</div>
            <div class="index-prose small">
              <p>
                <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hacker" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hacking</a>
                is the manipulation or exploration of most things computer-related, often online.
                But hacking is not to be confused with software cracking, theft, or ransomware.
                Originally, hacking had a more benign and innocent purpose of experimentation.
                Unfortunately, nowadays, with the involvement of nation-state bad actors and organized crime, hacking is often associated with digital privateering.
              </p>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section class="border rounded-3 p-3 bg-body-tertiary">
            <h3 class="h5 fw-semibold mb-1">Phreak</h3>
            <div class="font-monospace small text-body-secondary mb-2">phone phreaking &middot; blue boxing</div>
            <div class="index-prose small">
              <p>
                Today, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phreaking" target="_blank" rel="noopener">phone phreaking</a> is redundant,
                but it was once a popular method of exploiting the landline telephone networks to make free phone calls back when these were often billed by the minute and pricey.
              </p>
              <p>
                Phreaking was criminal but still popular with risk-taking computer users who were willing to use their home address to
                connect gratis to remote boards located outside of their local metropolitan area, as well as boards based interstate and overseas.
              </p>
              <p>
                Phreaking also allowed for "party lines". These were gratis group voice chats with people from around the world,
                long before they became commonplace with broadband internet.
              </p>
            </div>
          </section>
        </div>

        <div id="further-reading" class="border-top mt-4 pt-3 index-prose small" style="scroll-margin-top: 5rem;">
          <h3 class="h6 text-uppercase text-body-secondary fw-semibold mb-2">Further reading</h3>
          <p>
            As of 2022, a couple of professionally published books cover The Scene and its influences on modern online culture:
            <a href="https://punctumbooks.com/titles/warez-the-infrastructure-and-aesthetics-of-piracy/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Infrastructure and Aesthetics of Piracy</a>
            and <a href="https://yalebooks.yale.edu/book/9780300248142/modem-world/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Modem World: A Prehistory of Social Media</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            Author of <em>The Future Was Here: The Commodore Amiga</em>, <a href="https://mitpress.mit.edu/9780262535694/the-future-was-here/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jimmy Maher</a>,
            has written two excellent articles on early pirating and the pirate scenes on both the Apple II and Commodore 64 computers:
            <br>A Pirate's Life for Me, Part 1: <a href="https://www.filfre.net/2015/12/a-pirates-life-for-me-part-1-dont-copy-that-floppy/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Don't Copy That Floppy!</a>
            <br>A Pirate's Life for Me, Part 2: <a href="https://www.filfre.net/2016/01/a-pirates-life-for-me-part-2-the-scene/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Scene</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article id="the-creative" class="changelog-entry p-4">
        <div class="index-chapter-era font-monospace mb-1">Art and demos</div>
        <h2 class="h4 fw-semibold mb-3">The creative Scenes</h2>

        <div class="row g-3">
          <div class="col-md-6">
            <section class="border rounded-3 p-3 h-100 bg-body-tertiary">
              <h3 class="h5 fw-semibold mb-1">Art</h3>
              <div class="font-monospace small text-body-secondary mb-2">pixel &middot; raster &middot; vga &middot; ansi &middot; bbs art &middot; ascii</div>
              <p class="small mb-0">
                Art scene creations are a form of digital artworks drawn or painted using purpose-made software.
                The common forms of this expression are pixel art, ray-tracing art, monochrome text or ASCII art, and colored text ANSI art.
              </p>
            </section>
          </div>

          <div class="col-md-6">
            <section class="border rounded-3 p-3 h-100 bg-body-tertiary">
              <h3 class="h5 fw-semibold mb-1">Music</h3>
              <div class="font-monospace small text-body-secondary mb-2">chiptune &middot; mod &middot; streamer &middot; tracker</div>
              <p class="small mb-0">
                Songs of the Music Scene are music tracks composed using purpose-made home computer software,
                often <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tracker_(music_software)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">tracker software</a>,
                to create <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chiptune" target="_blank" rel="noopener">chiptune</a> or other genres of electronic music.
                In the early era of 8-bit and 16-bit microcomputers, these songs were often used to demonstrate the audio capabilities of the computer platform.
              </p>
            </section>
          </div>

          <div class="col-md-6">
            <section class="border rounded-3 p-3 h-100 bg-body-tertiary">
              <h3 class="h5 fw-semibold mb-1">Demoscene</h3>
              <div class="font-monospace small text-body-secondary mb-2">intros &middot; demos &middot; games</div>
              <p class="small mb-0">
                <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Demoscene" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Demoscene</a> participants combine the art scenes, the music scenes, and
                their own programming trickery to create visually appealing, non-interactive software demonstrations.
                Created to demonstrate the graphic and audio capabilities of the chosen computer platform and the technical
                programming skills of the creators. Nowadays, the Demoscene is more focused on art style and design.
              </p>
            </section>
          </div>

          <div class="col-md-6">
            <section class="border rounded-3 p-3 h-100 bg-body-tertiary">
              <h3 class="h5 fw-semibold mb-1">Emulation</h3>
              <div class="font-monospace small text-body-secondary mb-2">emulators</div>
              <p class="small mb-0">
                Emulation software mimics hardware or operating systems to allow the running of software designed for a different platform.
                For example, a Windows PC can use emulation software to run games created for the Commodore 64.
                Emulator software has existed since the earliest days of computing, but the Emulation Scene probably came about in the early 1990s,
                with hardware hackers who experimented with getting defunct video game console software to play on personal computers.
              </p>
            </section>
          </div>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article id="directories" class="changelog-entry p-4">
        <div class="index-chapter-era font-monospace mb-1">Resources</div>
        <h2 class="h4 fw-semibold mb-3">Community directories</h2>
        <div class="row g-3">
          <div class="col-md-6">
            <dl class="row g-2 mb-0">
              <dt class="col-sm-4"><a href="https://demozoo.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="fw-semibold">Demozoo</a></dt>
              <dd class="col-sm-8 text-body-secondary small mb-3">
                The world's largest database of Scene-produced productions covering all fields, including art, demos, music, people, groups, and BBSes.
              </dd>
              <dt class="col-sm-4"><a href="https://www.pouet.net/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="fw-semibold">Pouët</a></dt>
              <dd class="col-sm-8 text-body-secondary small mb-3">
                A database of Scene-produced productions, including demos, intros, and music.
              </dd>
              <dt class="col-sm-4"><a href="https://16colo.rs/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="fw-semibold">16colors</a></dt>
              <dd class="col-sm-8 text-body-secondary small mb-0">
                The online gallery for ASCII, ANSI, and other text-based art forms. It is probably the most active corner on the Internet for ASCII art and its artists.
              </dd>
            </dl>
          </div>
          <div class="col-md-6">
            <dl class="row g-2 mb-0">
              <dt class="col-sm-4"><a href="https://files.scene.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="fw-semibold">Scene.org</a></dt>
              <dd class="col-sm-8 text-body-secondary small mb-3">
                Most likely the largest repository of scene art on the Internet. Its primary purpose is to host files, so there is no fluff such as user ratings or reviews.
              </dd>
              <dt class="col-sm-4"><a href="https://text-mode.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="fw-semibold">text-mode.org</a></dt>
              <dd class="col-sm-8 text-body-secondary small mb-3">
                A Tumblr-style gallery that takes classical text art and looks at how it relates to a modern context.
              </dd>
              <dt class="col-sm-4"><a href="http://textfiles.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="fw-semibold">textfiles.com</a></dt>
              <dd class="col-sm-8 text-body-secondary small mb-0">
                The largest online resource of texts and information files, many written from the pre-Internet era.
                <span class="d-block mt-1">Note: textfiles.com only supports unencrypted HTTP, which triggers browser warnings.</span>
              </dd>
            </dl>
          </div>
        </div>
      </article>

    </div>
  </div>
</div>
